<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow prose">
    <h2>The story so far</h2>
    <p>I grew up in Eastbourne. And Glasgow. And Billericay. And Bexhill. (We got about.) Then, like a lot of people, I headed to London for work and stayed 13 years. There I built websites, kept computer systems upright and led technical teams for the big boys: the likes of Barclays, British Airways and HSBC.</p>
    <p>All told I've been doing IT for more than 25 years, which in computer years makes me roughly a dinosaur. But the sea kept calling, and eventually we moved back. Now I live here with my family and our cockapoo, and on a good day you'll find me sea swimming or walking on the South Downs.</p>
    <p>Technology is now part of everyday life: the doctor, the bank, the shopping, keeping in touch with family. Plenty of people don't need a salesperson or a helpline. They need someone patient who'll explain things properly, have a bit of a laugh and never make them feel silly. That's me.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-tint" aria-labelledby="legit-heading">
  <div class="container narrow prose">
    <h2 id="legit-heading">"This site is silly. Do you actually know what you're doing?"</h2>
    <p>Fair question. Yes! Under the comic fonts and the bad jokes there's a proper tech nerd, with the paperwork to prove it.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A BSc in Multimedia Computing.</strong> Back when "multimedia" meant a CD-ROM with a wobbly video on it.</li>
      <li><strong>More than 25 years in IT and digital.</strong> Websites, servers, networks, security, and the odd printer that just wanted attention.</li>
      <li><strong>13 years in London tech</strong>, building and running systems for the likes of Barclays, British Airways and HSBC. If it's good enough for the banks, it's good enough for your Wi-Fi.</li>
      <li><strong>Still learning.</strong> Tech changes every week, so I keep up with it. That way you don't have to.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The silliness is on purpose. Tech is stressful enough without being talked down to. You get the know-how of someone who's looked after systems for big banks and airlines, explained like a mate over a cuppa.</p>
  </div>
</section>

